The outside-read check now defaults to asking in interactive sessions, and piped or scripted runs behave exactly as before. Build, clippy with -D warnings, the fmt check and the full test suite all pass. It's committed as afbc0a81 with the title "Part of #879", and I've commented on #879 with the sha and the table below.

What changed (src/outside_read.rs)
• A new function, fence_on(value, interactive), decides whether the check is on. "Interactive" uses the same stdin-is-a-terminal test the check already used to choose between asking and refusing:

  | YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS | terminal | no terminal |
  |---|---|---|
  | 1 | on (asks) | on (refuses, as before) |
  | 0 (new opt-out) | off | off |
  | unset or anything else | on (new) | off, same as before |

• Where I differed from the plan: I didn't accept true/false as values. The existing test enabled_only_by_exact_one, which I left unedited, says "true" must not turn the check on. Keeping exact 1/0 also means a script that set =true still runs unchecked, as it does today. I wrote this down in the commit body.
• When tests run (cfg(test)), the default treats the session as non-interactive. Otherwise running cargo test by hand in a terminal could stop and wait at a y/n prompt.
• Wording fix: the refusal for an unreadable current directory began "YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=1 is active". That's no longer true when an interactive run reaches it with the variable unset. It now says "The outside-read fence (YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS) is active", and a new test checks it. The no-terminal refusal keeps its "=1", since it can only appear when the variable is exactly 1.
• Tests: new tests cover every row of the table above. As a check that they catch a regression, I forced the unset branch to return false (break, test, restore in one command). fence_on_by_env_and_population and interactive_unset_is_on failed by name. After the restore all 24 tests passed and no marker was left in the file.

Who still runs without the check: scripts/evolve.sh:728 and :735 feed yoyo its prompt from a file (< "$prompt_file"), so stdin isn't a terminal and the evolve loop stays unchecked. Nothing in scripts/ or .github/ sets the variable. Both facts are in the commit body.

Docs: permissions.md now describes the default for each kind of run, the 0/1 override and that only those exact values count. safety.md gets a short new section. I also updated a stale "Opt-in" comment in src/tool_wrappers.rs; it's a comment-only change, so the line count is the same.

Still open on #879: bash isn't covered and can still cat files outside the launch directory.

One cost for interactive users: a yes covers only that exact directory, not its subdirectories. Reading across many directories outside the project (dependency sources, for example) will ask several times. YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=0 turns it off.
